  "raw_text": "242\n\nRis takaia\n \n(v)\n \n–\n \nto rest\n \n/ descansar\n\nRug\n \n(a/t:\n \nRog\n,\n \nRuk\n)\n \ntakaia\n \n(Rug takaia)\n \n(v)\n \n–\n \nto fornicate, to commit immorality, to commit adultery\n \n/ fornicar,\ncometer inmoralidad, cometer adulterio\n\nSâp takaia\n \n(v)\n \n–\n \nto become quiet, to become still\n \n/ callarse, quedarse quieto\n\nSari takaia\n \n(v)\n \n–\n \nto become sad, to become discouraged\n \n/ ponerse triste, desanimarse\n\nSiknis takaia\n \n(v)\n \n–\n \nto become sick\n \n/ ponerse enfermo\n\n(Note/Nota:\n \ndict and dictu\n \nalso use\n \nSiknis daukaia\n \nbut this is not commonly used /\n \ndict\n \ny\n \ndictu\n \ntambién usan\n \nSiknis daukaia\n\npero esto no se usa comúnmente)\n\nSiknis taki pruaia\n \n(v)\n \n–\n \nto become sick and die\n \n/ enfermarse y morir\n\nStadi takaia\n \n(v)\n \n–\n \nto study\n \n/ estudiar\n\nSilp stadi takaia\n \n(v)\n \n–\n \nto do personal study\n \n/ hacer estudio personal\n\n(Ex/Ej:\n \nsilp stadi takaia nitka ba\n \n–\n \nthe need to do personal study\n \n/ la necesidad de hacer un estudio personal)\n\nStadi takaia ulbanka\n \n(n/s)\n \n–\n \nstudy note\n \n/ nota de estudio\n\n(Note/Nota:\n \nnwtsty\n \nstadi takaia ulbanka:\n \nUsed to refer to study notes in the New World\n \nTranslation / Se usa para\n\nreferirse a notas de estudio en la Traducción del Nuevo Mundo)\n\nSwira takaia\n \n(v)\n \n–\n \nto become ashamed, to become embarrassed\n \n/\n \nllegara a avergonzarse, estar\n \navergonzado\n\nTaka\n \n(3) (n-connective construct of Takaia / s-construct conexivo de Takaia)\n \n–\n \noccurrence, happening, event,\nsomething that happened\n \n/ suceso, hecho, acontecimiento, algo que paso\n\n(Ex/Ej\n):\n \n¿Dîa taka mita King Ezequías ba sari pali inan?\n \n–\n \nBecause of what happening did King Hezekiah weep?\n \n/\n \n¿Por qué\n\nsuceso lloró\n \nel rey Ezequías\n \ndesconsoladamente\n?\n)\n\nTakbia kra\n \n(v)\n \n–\n \nmay become, may happen\n \n/ puede suceder, puede convertirse\n\n(Ex/Ej(\nfy15\n):\n \ndîa trabilka takbia kra\n \n–\n \nwhat trouble may happen\n \n/ que problema puede pasar)\n\nTaki waia\n \n(v)\n \n–\n \nto leave, to depart\n \n/ salir, partir\n\nTâski takaia\n \n(v)\n \n–\n \nto become dirty, to become unclean, to become filthy, to become untidy\n \n/ volverse sucio,\nvolverse desordenado\n\nTayat takaia\n \n(v)\n \n–\n \nto become bored, to become tired\n \n/ aburrirse, cansarse\n\nTim takaia\n \n(v)\n \n–\n \nto become tame\n \n/ volverse manso\n\nTrabil taki lukaia\n \n(v)\n \n–\n \nto think about trouble happening, to think about problems happening, to worry, to be\nconcerned with, to be anxious\n \n/ pensar en los problemas que suceden, preocuparse, estar ansioso\n\nUya takras\n \n–\n \ndoes not happen much, does not often occur\n \n/ no pasa mucho, no ocurre a menudo\n\nWari takaia\n \n(v)\n \n–\n \nto become anxious, to become worried\n \n/ estar ansioso, preocuparse\n, llegar a estar preocupado\n\nWari taki ba\n \n(n/s)\n \n–\n \nconcern\n \n/ preocupación\n\n(Ex/Ej(\nth4\n):\n \nupla nani ...\n \ndîa dukiara\n \nwari taki ba\n \n–\n \nwhat people are worrying about\n \n/ de qué se preocupa la gente\n)\n\nWari takpara\n \n–\n \ndon’t worry, don’t be anxious\n \n/\n \nn\no te preocupes\n,\n \nno estés ansioso\n\nWark takaia\n \n(v)\n \n–\n \nto work\n \n/ trabajar\n\nAsla wark takaia\n \n(v)\n \n–\n \nto work unitedly together, to work in harmony together, to cooperate\n \n/ trabajar\nunidos, trabajar en armonía juntos, colaborar, cooperar\n\nWark tatakra\n \n(n-agent/s-agente)\n \n–\n \nworker\n \n/ trabajador\n\nDâkaia warkka tatakra\n \n(n-agent/s-agente)\n \n–\n \nharvest worker\n \n/ trabajador de la cosecha\n\n(Ex/Ej(\nlfb78\n):\n \nJehova ra puram suns dâkaia warkka tatakra nani kau blîkbia dukiara\n \n–\n \nPray to Jehovah to\nsend more harvest workers\n \n/ Oren a Jehová para que envíe más trabajadores de la cosecha)\n\nWark tatakra lankira\n \n–\n \nexperienced worker, skilled worker\n \n/ trabajador experimentado,\ntrabajador calificado\n\nWin takaia\n \n(v)\n \n–\n \nto win (at a game or in a battle)\n \n/ ganar (en un juego o en una batalla)\n\n(Note/Nota: This is used in\n \ndb-1 Timoti 6:19\n \nto translate of “\nto get a firm hold on\n”. However,\n \nPrak alkbia\n \n(to grap, to\ngrip...)\n \nis perhaps a better translation.\n \ns\nee/ver:\n \nbh\n-\npg192\n \n/ Esto se usa en\n \ndb\n-\n1\n \nTim\noti\n \n6:19\n \npara traducir\n \n\"para obtener un\n\nagarre firme\"\n. Sin embargo,\n \nPrak alkbia\n \n(agarrar ...) es\n \ntal vez\n \nuna mejor traducción. Ver\n \nbh\n-\npg192\n)\n\n(\nEx/Ej\n(\nwqu\n-\n08/19\n):\n \nDîa daukaia\n \nsa ki plapanka ba win takaia\n \ndukiara?\n \n–\n \nWhat must we do in order to win the race?\n \n/ ¿Qué\n\ndebemos hacer para ganar la carrera?\n)\n\nWin takanka\n \n(n/s)\n \n–\n \nvictory, win\n \n/ victoria, triunfo\n\nWitnis takaia\n \n(v)\n \n–\n \nto witness, to testify\n \n/ presenciar, testificar\n\nYus takaia\n \n(v)\n \n–\n \nto get used to, to become accustomed, to get into the habit\n \n/ acostumbrarse",
